          ":question/problem": "To which of the following sets does the number $\\pi $ belong?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/67/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $N$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $Z$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $Q$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $R$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following:\n\n- The number $\\pi $ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $Z$ nor to $N,$ so we can write\n\n$$\n\\pi \\notin N,\\,\\pi \\notin Z.\n$$\n\n- Also, notice that $\\pi =3.14159\\,\u2026\\,$ can not be written as the ratio of two integers, so it does *not* belong to $Q$ either, and we can write \n\n$$\n\\pi \\notin Q.\n$$\n\n- Every irrational number is both a real number and a complex number. Consequently, $\\pi $ belongs to both $R$ and $C,$ and we can write\n\n$$\n\\pi \\in R,\\,\\pi \\in C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $C$ only.\""
        },
