        ":topic/id": "d7019ce6-160a-5aa4-9e08-78782f485dfa",
        ":topic/knowledge-points": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054848,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054425,
              ":question/id": "16e0f524-49b5-549d-8e60-8597379c4350",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13134",
              ":question/problem": "List the elements of the following set: \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{a, c,3, \\{c,3 \\}, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}\\big\\}\n$$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "The elements of the set $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\na, \\quad c, \\quad 3, \\quad \\{c,3\\}, \\quad \\emptyset, \\quad \\{\\emptyset\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\n- The elements $c,3,$ and $\\{c,3\\}$ are three separate entities. The first two are the elements $c$ and $3,$ and the third is a set *containing* these two elements.\n\n- The elements $\\emptyset$ and $\\{ \\emptyset \\}$ are also separate entities. The first one is the empty set (i.e., the set containing no elements), while the second is a set that contains the empty set."
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "33551349-bb39-5a79-bf56-a4df753201ed",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying the Elements of a Set"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054849,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054426,
              ":question/id": "046f7284-3715-555c-bdc9-c108db061205",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13135",
              ":question/problem": "Which of the following is **not** a set?\n\nI. $\\{ 2, 4, 6 \\}$\n\nII. $\\{ \\{ 2 \\}, \\{ 4, 6 \\} \\}$\n\nIII. $2, 4, 6$\n\nIV. $\\emptyset$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $\\emptyset.$\n\n- $\\{ 2, 4, 6 \\}$ is a set. The elements are $2,$ $4,$ and $6,$ and they are listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 2 \\}, \\{ 4, 6 \\} \\}$ is a set. The elements are $\\{ 2 \\}$ and $\\{ 4, 6 \\},$ and are listed between the two outermost braces.\n\n- $2, 4, 6$ is *not* a set. The numbers $2,$ $4,$ and $6$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\emptyset$ is the empty set. Therefore, it is a set.\n\nIn conclusion, only III is not a set."
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "de59db4d-82d5-5bad-a559-bf5396375355",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying Non-Sets"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054850,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054427,
              ":question/id": "5404d545-570b-53d7-b16a-b23a371d4abd",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13136",
              ":question/problem": "Write the following statement using element notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ and $2$ are elements of a set $S,$ but $3,$ $4,$ and $5$ are not.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a \\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a \\notin S.$\n\n- Since $1$ and $2$ are elements of $S,$ we write $1,2 \\in S.$\n\n- Since $3,$ $4,$ and $5$ are *not* elements of $S,$ we write $3,4,5 \\notin S.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$$\n1,2 \\in S\\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad 3,4,5 \\notin S\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "900f0d3d-6a5c-53b2-b987-eee1a8963b6b",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Using Element Notation"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186054851,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186054428,
              ":question/id": "0684e366-bcf4-5b08-8eae-6600a88e2920",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-13137",
              ":question/problem": "Express the following sets using an ellipsis:\n\n- The set $X$ of all positive integers greater than or equal to $10$\n\n- The set $Y$ of integers less than zero\n\n- The set $A$ of all natural numbers greater than $5$ and less than $5\\, 005$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element, or \"and so on\" if not.\n\n- To express the set $X,$ we start with the first positive integer greater than or equal to $10,$ which is $10.$ The next integer is $11,$ the one after that is $12,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\nX = \\{10, 11, 12, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\n- To express the set $Y,$ we start with the first integer less than zero, which is $-1.$ The next integer less than zero is $-2,$ the one after that is $-3,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\nY = \\{ -1, -2, -3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that it is conventional to write a set of numbers from least to greatest, just like is shown on the number line. Writing the elements of $Y$ from least to greatest, we have\n\n$$\nY = \\{ \\ldots , -3, -2, -1\\}.\n$$\n\n- To express the set $A,$ we start with the first positive integer greater than $5,$ which is $6.$ The next integer is $7,$ the one after that is $8,$ and so on up to $5\\, 004$ (the largest integer less than $5\\, 005$). So, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\{6, 7, 8, \\ldots , 5004\\}.\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "5ceb6c84-a16b-5519-abd6-d2bb72ea81d3",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Identifying the Set Corresponding to a Description"
          }
        ],
