        ":topic/id": "3dfc702e-510d-534f-98bc-06c367122acf",
        ":topic/knowledge-points": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186133745,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186133555,
              ":question/id": "1e830784-49d8-5cc7-9703-d1a7513333e9",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4861",
              ":question/problem": "Which of the following numbers are irrational?\n\n1. $\\sqrt 3$\n2. $\\dfrac32$\n3. $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt2} 2$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "First, recall the following:\n\n- A rational number is any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n- Any real number is irrational if it is not rational.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt 3$ is an irrational number. It cannot be expressed as the ratio of two integers.\n- $\\dfrac32$ is not an irrational number. It is a rational number.\n- $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ is an irrational number. It cannot be expressed as the ratio of two integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "f0379ee2-f2ed-5a3f-b23d-b7175298b333",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Classifying Irrational and Real Numbers"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186133859,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186133672,
              ":question/id": "e1b87de2-abb4-57ec-8928-674dfa474935",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-8176",
              ":question/problem": "![](images/12/12f69e1bc4941538759b236578e6679b353b714974321eb4d9c7acbd14dc28ab.png)\n\nIn which section should the number $-\\sqrt{15}$ be placed in the diagram above?",
              ":question/worked-solution": "Let's go through each category from top to bottom:\n\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is a real number $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is not a rational number $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is not an integer $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is not a natural number $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct section of the diagram is IV (the real numbers)."
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "e108a956-ba00-5854-beaf-7d85d47d1397",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Placing a Real Number on a Diagram"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186133901,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186133717,
              ":question/id": "13b7c475-3f64-5cc0-9535-2aba67abe708",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-9591",
              ":question/problem": "Which of the following is an irrational number?\n\n$4, \\qquad 3.1415926 \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{5}{13}, \\qquad 1.27, \\qquad 5.7232323 \\ldots$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "An irrational number is a number that cannot be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of our numbers.\n\n- $4$ is a *rational* number. It can be written as $\\dfrac{4}{1}$.\n- $3.1415926 \\ldots$ is an irrational number. The digits after the decimal point in $3.1415926 \\ldots$ extend forever, but there is no regular pattern.\n- $\\dfrac{5}{13}$ is a *rational* number.\n- $1.27$ is a *rational* number since it is a terminating decimal.\n- $5.7232323\\ldots$ extends forever but has a repeating pattern ($23$ repeats forever). Therefore, it's a *rational* number.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only $3.1415926$ is an irrational number."
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "30895d97-eca9-5bc7-92f4-b9e4c6c3be11",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Classifying Decimals as Irrational"
          }
